{0 Diagnostics}

Use diagnostics when you want to keep parsing and report multiple validation
problems instead of stopping at the first one.

{1 Types}

{@ocaml skip[
module Diagnostics : sig
  type 'd entry = { pos : int; value : 'd }
  type 'd t
  val create : unit -> 'd t
  val to_list : 'd t -> 'd entry list
  val length : 'd t -> int
  val is_empty : 'd t -> bool
  val clear : 'd t -> unit
end
]}

A {!type:Parseff.Diagnostics.t} collects the diagnostics of one parse. Its type
parameter is the type of the values given to {!val:Parseff.warn}, and
{!val:Parseff.Diagnostics.to_list} returns the same type. The two cannot
differ.

{1 Emitting diagnostics}

{@ocaml skip[val warn : 'd Diagnostics.t -> 'd -> unit]}

{!val:Parseff.warn} records a non-fatal diagnostic at the current parser
position.

{@ocaml skip[val warn_at : 'd Diagnostics.t -> pos:int -> 'd -> unit]}

{!val:Parseff.warn_at} records a non-fatal diagnostic at an explicit position.

{1 Collecting diagnostics}

Create a collector and pass it to the runner with [~diagnostics]. The runner
empties it first. The parser must warn into the same collector. [warn] into a
collector that the runner did not get does nothing.

The result of the parse is the usual {!type:Parseff.error} result. Read the
diagnostics from the collector after the parse. They are there for [Ok] and for
[Error].

{@ocaml skip[val parse :
  ?max_depth:int ->
  ?consume:[ `All | `Partial ] ->
  ?diagnostics:'d Diagnostics.t ->
  string ->
  (unit -> 'a) ->
  ('a, error) Stdlib.result]}

{@ocaml skip[Parseff.parse input parser]}

By default the parse requires that all input is consumed. It is the same as
calling {!val:Parseff.end_of_input} once more at the end of your parser. It does
not change how explicit {!val:Parseff.end_of_input} calls behave inside your
parser. Pass [~consume:`Partial] to let the parse stop before the end of the
input.
{!val:Parseff.parse_source} takes the same arguments.

{1 Example}

{@ocaml[
type validation = [ `Octet_out_of_range of int ]

let number_lenient diagnostics () =
  let start = Parseff.position () in
  let digits = Parseff.many ~at_least:1 Parseff.digit () in
  let n = List.fold_left (fun acc d -> (acc * 10) + d) 0 digits in
  if n > 255 then
    Parseff.warn_at diagnostics ~pos:start (`Octet_out_of_range n);
  n

let ip_address_lenient diagnostics () =
  let a = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let b = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let c = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let d = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  (a, b, c, d)

let () =
  let diagnostics : validation Parseff.Diagnostics.t =
    Parseff.Diagnostics.create ()
  in
  let outcome =
    Parseff.parse ~diagnostics "999.2.3.256"
      (ip_address_lenient diagnostics)
  in
  (match outcome with
  | Ok (a, b, c, d) -> Printf.printf "Parsed: %d.%d.%d.%d\n" a b c d
  | Error error ->
      Printf.printf "Parse error at %d: %s\n" error.range.start
        (Parseff.error_message error));
  List.iter
    (fun ({ pos; value } : validation Parseff.Diagnostics.entry) ->
      match value with
      | `Octet_out_of_range n ->
          Printf.printf "  at %d: octet %d out of range (0-255)\n" pos n)
    (Parseff.Diagnostics.to_list diagnostics)
]}

{1 Backtracking semantics}

Diagnostics are transactional with parser control flow:

- {!val:Parseff.or_}: diagnostics from failed branches are rolled back
- {!val:Parseff.many}: diagnostics from the final failing attempt are rolled
  back
- {!val:Parseff.look_ahead}: diagnostics are rolled back

This prevents diagnostics from leaking out of speculative branches.

{1 Ref}

A {!type:Parseff.Ref.t} is a [ref] whose changes are undone when the parser
backtracks.

{@ocaml skip[
module Ref : sig
  type 'a t
  val create : 'a -> 'a t
  val get : 'a t -> 'a
  val set : 'a t -> 'a -> unit
end
]}

Use it for a value that the parser builds as it reads, such as a symbol table,
an indentation stack or a counter. A plain [ref] is wrong for this. When a
branch fails, the parser goes back and tries the next branch, but the [ref]
keeps the changes of the failed branch.

In this example both branches of [statement] call [declare]. On ["abc;"], the
first branch reads [abc] and fails on [;], then the second branch reads [abc]
again. The [ref] holds the name twice. The {!type:Parseff.Ref.t} holds it once,
because the parser undid the [set] of the failed branch.

{@ocaml[
let plain = ref []
let names = Parseff.Ref.create []

let declare () =
  let name =
    Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= 'a' && c <= 'z')
  in
  plain := name :: !plain;
  Parseff.Ref.set names (name :: Parseff.Ref.get names)

let statement () =
  Parseff.or_
    (fun () ->
      declare ();
      let _ = Parseff.char '=' in
      `Assign)
    (fun () ->
      declare ();
      let _ = Parseff.char ';' in
      `Declare)
    ()

let () =
  plain := [];
  Parseff.Ref.set names [];
  match Parseff.parse "abc;" statement with
  | Ok `Declare ->
      assert (!plain = [ "abc"; "abc" ]);
      assert (Parseff.Ref.get names = [ "abc" ])
  | _ -> assert false
]}

{2 When a set is undone}

A {!val:Parseff.Ref.set} is undone when its branch backtracks. The rule is the
same as for diagnostics:

- the failed branch of {!val:Parseff.or_} and {!val:Parseff.one_of};
- a failed {!val:Parseff.optional};
- the last iteration of {!val:Parseff.many}, {!val:Parseff.sep_by} and
  {!val:Parseff.fold_left}, the one that fails or consumes nothing;
- {!val:Parseff.look_ahead}, also when it succeeds.

A [set] is kept when its branch succeeds. After {!val:Parseff.commit} or
{!val:Parseff.cut} seals a branch, a failure no longer undoes the [set]s in
that branch. An outer branch that backtracks still undoes them.

An exception that escapes a branch skips the undo. A {!val:Parseff.fail}, an
{!val:Parseff.error} or an exception of your own raised inside
{!val:Parseff.or_}, {!val:Parseff.optional}, {!val:Parseff.many},
{!val:Parseff.sep_by} or {!val:Parseff.fold_left} leaves the cell with the value
it had when the exception was raised. {!val:Parseff.look_ahead} restores the
value also in this case. Diagnostics behave the same way.

Only the value in the cell is restored. A mutable value inside the cell, such
as a [Hashtbl.t] or an array, keeps its changes. Store immutable values, such
as a [Map] or a list.

{2 Lifetime}

Create a cell anywhere, inside or outside the parser. A parse does not reset
the cell. The value at the end of a parse stays in it, also when the parse
fails, so set the start value before each parse. Outside a parse,
{!val:Parseff.Ref.get} and {!val:Parseff.Ref.set} work as for a [ref].

{2 Cost}

A [set] inside a branch that can backtrack allocates 6 words to keep the old
value. The old value stays in memory while an open branch can undo it. A [set]
in each iteration of a {!val:Parseff.many} at the top of the parser keeps one
old value. A grammar inside one {!val:Parseff.or_} keeps every old value until
that {!val:Parseff.or_} ends. A parser that never calls [set] allocates nothing
more.

{2 Errors}

{!val:Parseff.Ref.set} raises [Invalid_argument] when another parse that is
still running set the same cell. This happens when:

- two parses run interleaved on one domain, for example through effects in a
  {!val:Parseff.Source.of_function} reader;
- you call [set] outside a parse while such a reader waits, on a cell that the
  waiting parse set;
- you call {!val:Parseff.parse} inside a parser, and both parses set the cell.

Give each parse its own cell. A cell is not safe to share between domains.
